kind: graph
semantic_diff: >-
  Node merges, user-directed, fable adversarial review 2026-09-11 (2 KEEP, 6 MODIFY
  with the named fixes applied, 1 REJECT honored). 32 nodes become 22; 96 edges
  become 68; 89 outcomes become 68; checks stay 17 (closure_evidence_settled removed,
  changeset_complete added). Merges - (1) screen_pin_and_progress, draft_increment_plan,
  assemble_regression_matrix, preregister_acceptance, assemble_design_record become
  design_campaign with outcomes design_ready, infeasibility_recorded, design_blocked;
  the comparator commitment moves to the design_ready exit edge and the infeasibility
  guard to the infeasibility exit edge; the node may run sequential seats under one
  id. (2) assemble_delta_report folds into cost_routes_and_rank_backlog; evidence_gap
  folds into reports_insufficient. (3) record_changeset is removed; scope_next_increment
  assembles the changeset on plan_satisfied and the lead check changeset_complete
  routes a gap back to scoping with its class. (4) acquire_hardware_lease and
  replicate_campaign_venv become prepare_host with full and probe-only entry modes.
  (5) run_candidate_measurements and stabilize_and_package_evidence become
  measure_candidate; revision_stamped moves to its exit edge. (6) realize_measurement_procedures
  and capture_baseline_reference become prepare_measurements with both SSH permissions
  kept verbatim. (7) review_measurement_verdict is removed; adjudicate_results names
  the consequence (gates_passed, correctness_shortfall, no_benefit, regression_failed);
  its no_progress outcome is removed; review_pr_evidence now consumes the verdict;
  the review principle no longer lists measurement verdicts. (8) verify_run_closure
  becomes a lead-only node recording world-issued identifiers (PR number on the fork,
  commit hash of the artifact update); the duplicate check closure_evidence_settled
  is removed. review_implementation is kept unchanged (merge 9 rejected). All routes,
  recovery paths, role boundaries, and P1-P13 mechanisms survive.

  PART TWO - LEASES AS RESERVATIONS, QUEUE AS A TOOL (folded into this same
  revision at the user's word, 2026-09-11: "just put it in revision 11"; the
  merge part above was reviewed in rounds 1-3, the lease part in rounds 5-6, by
  the same fable reviewer). Combined diff against revision 10: 48 hunks,
  703 added lines against 1216 removed.

  A hardware lease becomes a reservation of named non-shareable pools on a
  host, never the whole host, and the hardware queue becomes a tool the
  requesting seat invokes, not the lead. This revision also ports the live run's proposal v10
  (drafted by pave-init:workflow-updater against revision 9, passed by
  pave-init:update-reviewer round 1, requested by the user 2026-09-11 ~15:00Z:
  grants only for jobs that consume non-shareable resources - N Neuron devices,
  compile RAM - so jobs run in parallel when capacity is free) onto the 22-node
  graph of this same revision, and adds the one change the field evidence asks for beyond it -
  the lead leaves the grant loop. The lease part is prose inside the merged structure - 9
  hunks, 107 added lines against 44 removed, 2272 to 2335 lines. Node ids, outcomes,
  required_evidence, edges, checks, evidence, control nodes, write limits, and
  the state.required list are equal field by field (proved by parsing both
  graphs); two role lists change (the lead leaves prepare_host and
  recover_leased_host) and two state fields gain a meaning (instance_roster,
  hardware_lease_record).

  LAYER TEST (pave-spec 2.1). What a lease means, who may write a lease record,
  what host_ready and no_host_available settle on, and what a node may do on a
  shared host are graph meaning - kind graph. Pool sizes, the lease request
  form, the lock file, and the queue tool's command line are Runtime Binding
  (run inputs in instance_roster; a plugin script shipped with this release) -
  the graph names their classes only.

  (1) LEASE = RESERVATION, TWO GRAINS, ONE NOUN (prepare_host purpose, host_ready,
  no_host_available). The campaign lease reserves nothing and binds the
  campaign to the host's verified identity; every job that takes non-shareable
  capacity holds its own job lease naming the pools and amounts - Neuron
  devices, compile memory, CPU cores for a long compile or a many-worker
  start-up (the r14 serving start-up lost its rendezvous window to a concurrent
  CPU-only test, so cores are a pool the roster may size), the compile-cache
  write slot as a pool of one - and starts when they fit the remaining capacity,
  derived from open job leases, never a stored counter. Jobs of different
  campaigns share a host when their reservations fit; "exclusive", "leased
  elsewhere", and "claimed" leave the text. A job that reserves nothing holds no
  job lease and runs under the campaign lease on its own identity probe and
  attempt record; P8 still counts it through the attempt records the loop writes.

  (2) THE QUEUE IS A TOOL, NOT THE LEAD (prepare_host purpose, activities,
  effects; execute_attempt_loop and recover_leased_host effects; lead role
  purpose; roles at two nodes). Field evidence (v10 usage read, 2026-09-11): 291 lease
  records against 0 acquire-seat traversals - the lead ran the queue by hand, one
  chat round trip per grant and per release (~10 KB each), every job CPU-only.
  Now the hardware queue tool is the single writer of every lease record -
  grant, release, amendment, one file per event - taking the grant under a
  per-host lock so two jobs never both get the last unit; the requesting seat
  invokes it and never writes a record by hand; the lead freezes the roster at
  intake, reads the records for scheduling, and arbitrates nothing per grant.
  P10 narrows to run state and the four cross-run artifacts; one writer per
  record (pave-spec 8.2) holds, the writer is a tool. The lead leaves the roles
  of prepare_host and recover_leased_host because no lead action remains in
  either node.

  (3) SHARED-HOST RECOVERY AND CHARGING (recover_leased_host purpose, first
  activity, effects; execute_attempt_loop host_faulted). One recovery per host
  at a time - the first recorded fault owns it; co-tenants wait for the owner's
  record, re-verify identity, and exit with the owner's outcome through the
  edges they already have; every campaign that faulted in the episode consumes
  its allowance. A whole-host action while another campaign's job lease is open
  is a forbidden effect. Every attempt in flight at the fault is host-faulted and
  uncharged except one whose fault evidence names its own use beyond its job
  lease - a failed attempt, fingerprinted and charged.

  (4) STATE MEANINGS (instance_roster, hardware_lease_record). The roster is a
  run input frozen at intake with pool sizes; an unsized pool is indivisible, so a
  roster that sizes no pools behaves as one job per host and the revision
  degrades gracefully. The lease record field points at the one-file-per-event
  records the tool writes; remaining capacity is derived, never stored.

  ROUND-5 REVIEW FIXES (fable reviewer, 2026-09-11): a co-tenant entering
  recovery releases its open job leases first, so the owner's reboot is never
  barred by a dead attempt's lease; the no-lease rule is one test everywhere -
  a job holds no job lease only when it takes nothing the roster sizes; lease
  and recovery records are keyed by host and readable by every campaign leasing
  it, a boot-identifier amendment applies to every campaign lease naming the
  host, and the boot-identifier delta reads any campaign's recovery records for
  that host; a leaked grant (attempt record closed, no release) is released by
  the tool on its next invocation; an oversize reservation is a request defect,
  corrected and re-requested, never a wait; runtime restart is a whole-host
  action; the charging exception reads the transcript or fault telemetry. ROUND-6 FIX (same reviewer): the no-lease test is
  "takes no pool" - a job that needs a pool the roster leaves unsized reserves
  the host whole instead of skipping its lease, so a roster that sizes no pools
  really does behave as one job per host; the recovery allowed effect covers
  the co-tenant's faulted-attempt releases; lease and recovery records are
  both readable across campaigns.

  NOT DONE. No refusal for capacity that exists but is busy - a job that does
  not fit now waits (scheduling is Runtime Binding; a refusal would need an
  outcome and edge no field case asked for). No hook - over-use of a reservation is detectable only after the
  fact from host telemetry; the rung is prose plus record (lease record, use in
  the transcript, fault evidence naming the over-user), recorded with its reason
  (pave-spec 9.14.1). Dependent text lands with the release, not here: SKILL.md
  P10 row and stage row 7 ("lease records are lead-written"), roles table,
  agents/implementer.md prepare_host and recovery bullets, artifact-layout 4.2
  pair 7 (job, pools, amounts, release added), run-state schema
  hardware_lease_record description, and the new scripts/hardware_queue.py
  (grant, release, amend, status; per-host lock; one file per event).
