changelog_entry: >-
  Revision 11 (graph): 32 nodes merged to 22 - new nodes design_campaign,
  prepare_host, prepare_measurements, measure_candidate; adjudicate_results
  names the consequence directly; verify_run_closure is lead-only with
  world-issued identifiers; the lead assembles the changeset under the
  changeset_complete check. In the same revision, a hardware lease reserves
  named pools (Neuron devices, compile memory, CPU cores, the compile-cache
  write slot) from the roster's declared capacity, never the whole host; a
  zero-reservation job holds no job lease; jobs of different campaigns share a
  host when their reservations fit; the hardware queue tool, not the lead,
  writes every lease record under a per-host lock, and the lead leaves the
  roles of prepare_host and recover_leased_host. Outcomes, edges, effects, and
  role lists change, so this revision carries the user's approval.
approval: >-
  User, 2026-09-11, on the merge: "approve revision 11, but run-state.json would
  need to be updated right? how does that happen". On the lease model, to the
  run lead about 15:00Z: "use /pave-init:pave-evolve to explore updating grants
  to only apply for jobs that consume non-sharable resources (i.e. multiple
  agents want to use N neuron devices, if there is available, then they can
  run in parallel.) This could also apply for RAM (i.e. compilations that take
  X amount of memory but don't want to exceed host capacity). CPU tests are
  rarely applicable since they're not long running". On folding both into one
  revision: "just put it in revision 11".
